    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-001", "title": "1", "content": "1\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nA Decade \nof Progress \nNayib Rivera\nCarole Chartouni\nEkaterina Pankratova\nJohannes Koettl\nTjaša Bartolj\nMiha Dominko\nSimon Bilo\nAhmed Alzahrani\nAhmed HumediINSIDE SAUDI ARABIA’S\nLABOR MARKET\nTRANSFORMATION\nPublic Disclosure Authorized\nPublic Disclosure Authorized\nPublic Disclosure Authorized\nPublic Disclosure Authorized\n\n2\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\n→ →\n→\n→→\n→→\n→→\n→→ →→ →\n→\n→→\n→→\n→→\n→→ →→ →\n→\n→→\n→→\n→→\n→→ →→ →\n→\n→→\n→→\n→→\n→→ →→ →\n→\n→→\n→→\n→→\n→→ →→ →\n→\n→→\n→→\n→→\n→→ →→ →\n→\n→→\n→→\n→→\n", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-002", "title": "3", "content": "3\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThis report offers fresh insights into Saudi Arabia’s labor market transfor -\nmation over the past decade, highlighting some of the accomplishments in \nreshaping employment outcomes achieved after implementation of Vision \n2030 and the National Labor Market Strategy. Leveraging a rich dataset \ncomparing 2025 with a 2015 pre–Vision 2030 baseline, it offers details about \nsome of the main policy reforms that the Ministry of Human Resources and \nSocial Development has led and how these reforms have helped modernize \nlabor m", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-003", "title": "4", "content": "4\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\n© [2026] The World Bank\n1818 H Street NW, Washington DC 20433\nTelephone: 202-473-1000; Internet: www.worldbank.org \n \nSome rights reserved.\n \nThis work was prepared by The World Bank at the request of the Ministry \nof Human Resources and Social Development in Saudi Arabia. The findings, \ninterpretations, and conclusions expressed in this work do not necessarily \nreflect the views of the Executive Directors of The World Bank or the gov -\nernments they represent.\n \nThe World Bank does not guarantee the accuracy of the data", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-004", "title": "5", "content": "5\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nExecutive Summary  / 9\n \nBackground  / 12\nSaudi Arabia’s Transformation Journey / 12\nVision 2030 and the LMS / 14 \nAccelerated and Inclusive Human \nCapital Activation  / 23\nA More Dynamic Private Sector  / 32\nBreaking the New Frontier:  \nWhat’s next for Saudi Arabia  / 53\n \nReferences / 57\nEndnotes / 58Contents\n →\n →\n →\n →\n →", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-005", "title": "6", "content": "6\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nTable of figures\nFigure 1.  Percentage of Saudi  Nationals Employed According to Sex, Age, and Education  / 24\nFigure 2.  Labor Force Participation Rates & Not in Employment, Education or Training (NEET)  / 25\nFigure 3.  Job Transitions Out of Inactivity for Youth and Women  / 26\nFigure 4.  Share of Respondents Reporting That They Would Rather Be at Home Than Go to Work According to \nSex and Age  / 26\nFigure 5.  Acceptance of Saudi Women Working in Mixed-Gender Workplaces  / 27\nFigure 6.  Sectoral Distribution of Employm", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-006", "title": "7", "content": "7\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThe report benefited from the technical insights and \nstrategic guidance of Johannes  Koettl– Brodmann \n(Senior Economist and Task Team Leader for Gulf \nCooperation Council Social Protection and Labor \nEngagement),  Cristobal Ridao–Cano (Practice Mana-  \nger, Social Protection and  Labor, Middle East and \nNorth  Africa,  Pakistan, and Afghanistan), Safaa El \nTayeb El–Kogali (Gulf Cooperation Council Divi-\nsion Director), and Steffen Soulejman Janus (Riyadh \n Regional Hub Manager). \nThe World Bank task team expresses its ", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-007", "title": "A Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation", "content": "A Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\n8\nPhoto credit: © AdobeStock / Mehran", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-008", "title": "9", "content": "9\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nExecutive \nSummary\nOver the past decade, Saudi Arabia’s Vision 2030 and the National La -\nbor Market Strategy (LMS) have noticeably improved the country’s labor \nmarket—setting a national blueprint for shifting the economy beyond oil \nby catalyzing private sector job creation; boosting labor force participation, \nespecially of women and youth; and equipping workers for a digital economy. \nThis report highlights some of the key reforms and achievements in labor \nmarket outcomes in the past decade, leveraging new datasets ", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-009", "title": "10", "content": "10\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\n→ Changing social norms:  Saudis’ views about work, particularly \nwork in the private sector, have improved, helping close the \npreference gap with the public sector, with a large share of \nunemployed individuals seeking jobs in the private sector. In \naddition, social norms regarding which workplaces are acces-\nsible to women have changed considerably, with a substantial \nincrease in support for women working in mixed-gender settings.\n \n \n \nA comprehensive mix of reforms contributed to these achievements.  Saudi \nArabi", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-010", "title": "11", "content": "11\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\n11", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-011", "title": "12", "content": "12\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nBackground\nSaudi Arabia’s  \n Transformation Journey\nSaudi Arabia is gradually introducing changes to its labor market and \neconomy as it seeks to diversify and modernize its systems. The oil-rich \ncountry is pursuing an ambitious reform agenda to transition away from heavy \neconomic dependence on fossil fuels and toward a diversified, knowledge-in -\ntensive, competitive and diversified economy (Box 1). Broad reform packages \ncover all aspects of social and economic life, from modernizing institutions \nto building a thri", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-012", "title": "13", "content": "13\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nSaudi Arabia’s economy has remained resilient \ndespite external shocks and a decrease in oil \nrevenues, with inflation contained and unem -\nployment at record lows, especially for women \nand youth. Although temporary oil production \ncuts have weighed on growth, the outlook is \nstrengthening, with real gross domestic prod-\nuct (GDP) projected to increase by 3.6 percent \nin 2025 and 3.9 percent in 2026 as oil output \nnormalizes.2\nThe economic structure has shifted markedly \nfrom oil dependence toward non-oil expansion, \nw", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-013", "title": "14", "content": "14\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nSaudi Arabia’s Vision 2030 is a national plan to diversify the economy \nbeyond oil and raise living standards, with a strong emphasis on labor \nmarket reforms critical to national transformation. The Vision 2030 \ninitiatives designed to address labor market challenges can be categorized \ninto three main areas: modernizing governance and institutions; increasing \nemployment creation and inclusion; and improving skills, productivity, and \njob quality in the private sector. \n \nGovernance and institutions:  Robust governanc", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-014", "title": "15", "content": "15\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nTo achieve these ambitious Vision 2030 goals for the labor market, Saudi \nArabia embarked on a whole ‑of‑government effort led by the Ministry of \nHuman Resources and Social Development (MHRSD) to transform the \nSaudi labor market. The government brought together a diverse group of \nstakeholders representing all the ecosystem (e.g., business sector, government, \nglobal experts, knowledge institutions) to produce a comprehensive framework \nto address persistent challenges and improve outcomes: the LMS, which set \nambitio", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-015", "title": "16", "content": "16\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nEmployment \ninclusion & \nparticipation\n \n \n \n \n \nPrivate sector \njob quality \nand human \ncapital\n Facilitate inclusive participation \nof Saudi nationals from all \nsegments  \n \nEnhance working and \ncontracting environment  \n \n \nDevelop economic sectors and \nregions, encourage small and \nmedium-sized enterprises, \nattract foreign investment\n \nAttract talent while addressing \ngap between labor market needs \nand available skills \n \n \nAlign skills with evolving current \nand future labor market needs\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nEnhance mat", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-016", "title": "17", "content": "17\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThe LMS marked a pivotal step in transforming Saudi Arabia’s labor mar -\nket. Its success was rooted in its ability to bring together diverse stakeholders \nunder a unified vision, leverage robust data and global best practices, and \nestablish clear mechanisms for accountability and performance monitoring. \nBy fostering collaboration, learning, and adaptation, the strategy not only \naddressed immediate labor market challenges, but also laid the groundwork \nfor sustainable, long-term development. The experience demonstrat", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-017", "title": "18", "content": "18\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nAn evidence ‑based approach was fundamental for the design of the LMS. \nCountry-level diagnostics and design processes were grounded in rigorous \nanalytics and reliable data. Data served as the core knowledge base, providing \nempirical evidence to guide decision making. Access to multiple adminis -\ntrative data sources enabled robust, timely analytics, supporting ongoing \nmonitoring and evaluation. In particular, the Three Labor Market Surveys \n(3LMS) initiative generated a wealth of nationally representative labor mark", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-018", "title": "19", "content": "19\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThe LMS is on track to meet its 2030 targets, surpassing key targets for \n2025 (Table 2). As of mid-2025, Saudi Arabia’s labor market was outperforming \nits high-level near-term goals. Labor force participation was 67.1percent, well \nabove the 2025 target of 61.8 percent and already exceeding the 2030 goal of \n63.0 percent. The female labor force participation target for 2030 was achieved \nin 2022. The overall unemployment rate had declined to 2.8 percent, beating \nthe 2025 target of 4.6 percent and the 2030 target of 3", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-019", "title": "20", "content": "20\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThe national and expatriate surveys sampled the working-age population \n(18–64 years), and the firms target all sizes and economic activities. Al -\nthough Saudi Arabia’s experience offers key lessons, its unique country \ncontext might limit close replication and applicability. Countries with fewer \nresources, weaker governance, or constrained institutional capacity may \nfind it challenging to sequence, finance, and implement such an integrated \nagenda. Individual instruments can be adapted, but the most transferable \nin", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-020", "title": "21", "content": "21\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nBackgroundTable 3. Timeline of Selected Policy Reform Areas and Initiatives\n10/2008\n01/2011\n12/2014\n03/2016\n01/2017\n01/2017\n07/2017\n10/2017\n10/2017  \n11/2017\n12/2017\n01/2018\n01/2018\n01/2018\n04/2018\n08/2018\n12/2018\n01/2020\n04/2020\n08/2020\n11/2020\n11/2020\n01/2021\n01/2021\n01/2021\n03/2021\n03/2021\n04/2021\n08/2021\n09/2021\n04/2022  \n10/2022\n01/2023\n05/2023\n03/2024\n07/2024\n10/2024\n12/2024\n03/2025\n07/2025\n08/2025Skills\nLocalization\nSkills\nLocalization\nGender reforms\nGender reforms\nSkills\nFlexible work\nFlexible work  \nGender refo", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-021", "title": "22", "content": "22\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nChapter 1. Background22", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-022", "title": "23", "content": "23\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\n Accelerated \nand Inclusive \n Human Capital \nActivation \nA large share of Saudi Arabia’s workforce consists of prime ‑working ‑age \n(25–54) foreign workers, alongside Saudi nationals who are also  primarily \nof prime working age. About 30.2 percent of Saudis are aged 18 to 24, and \n61.5 percent are in their prime working years (25–54 years). Among expa -\ntriates, the structure is even more concentrated in the most-productive \nyears, with 81.9 percent aged 25 to 54, 13.6 percent aged 18 to 24, and just \n4.5 percent aged ", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-023", "title": "24", "content": "24\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nFigure 1.  Percentage of Saudi \n Nationals Employed According to Sex, \nAge, and Education, 2015 and 2025\n   2025\n 2015\nSource: 3LMS Nationals70%\n60%\n50%\n40%\n30%\n20%\n10%\n0%\nMale\nGender Age EducationFemale Youth  \n(18-24)Prime-age  \n(25-54)Older workers\n(55+)Primary Secondary Tertiary\nThese demographic advantages can only translate into economic gains if \nsufficient people have access to employment opportunities and contribute \nproductively. As Saudi Arabia continues to implement labor market reforms \nand shift toward pri", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-024", "title": "25", "content": "25\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nAccelerated and Inclusive  Human Capital Activation \nFigure 2.  Labor Force \nParticipation Rates & Not in \nEmployment, Education or \nTraining (NEET)\n   2025\n 2015\nSource: 3LMS Nationals70%\n60%\n50%\n40%\n30%\n20%\n10%\n0%\nMale\nGender\nLabor Force Participation Rate NEETAge EducationFemale Youth (18-24) Primary Secondary Tertiary\nRising employment has broadened labor market inclusion of vulnerable \ngroups. The increase in job opportunities has resulted in a notable decline \nin the number of individuals excluded from the labor m", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-025", "title": "26", "content": "26\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nFigure 4.  Share of Respondents \nReporting That They Would Rather \nBe at Home Than Go to Work \nAccording to Sex and Age, 2015 \nand 2025\n   2025\n 2015\nSource: 3LMS NationalsFigure 3.  Job Transitions Out of \nInactivity for Youth and Women, \n2012 to 2015 and 2022 to 2025\n   Into Employment\n   Into Unemployment\n         Out of Inactivity\nSource: 3LMS Nationals30%\n25%\n20%\n15%\n10%\n5%\n0%\nYouth  \n(18-24)38%\n59%59%\n34%34%27%\n49%\n22%Saudi \nWomen\nSaudi  \nMenWomen Young Individuals (21-34) All2012-2015 2012-2015 2012-2015 2022-202", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-026", "title": "27", "content": "27\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nRapidly changing social norms are opening workplaces to women. There is \na noticeable decline in the proportion of Saudi men and women who believe \nthat women should work solely from home or within female–only workplaces \nor sections. The percentage of men supporting employment exclusively in \nfemale–only environments has decreased from 43% in 2015 to 21% in 2025. \nSimilarly, among Saudi women, this view has reduced from 44% to 11% over \nthe same period. As mixed–gender workplaces become increasingly common, \nemployers ", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-027", "title": "28", "content": "28\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nIn the past decade, Saudi Arabia has made sub -\nstantial strides in advancing women’s economic \nparticipation by removing numerous barriers to \nwork. Notable legal and labor law reforms have \ninfluenced women’s labor supply decisions. \nRecent labor market and gender reforms have \nenabled more Saudi women, including single \nand married mothers, to enter the workforce at \nhigher rates. Of these groups, single mothers \nhave seen particularly remarkable gains. In many high-income countries, working mothers \nare a vital segm", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-028", "title": "29", "content": "29\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nGender reforms have accelerated already ‑changing social norms. A range \nof gender reforms has been introduced, with the objective of breaking down \nbarriers and encouraging participation of Saudi women. The government’s \nemphasis on expanding women’s economic empowerment under Vision \n2030, together with recent gender reforms, has exposed genuine attitudes \ntoward women working, paving the way for them to work.11 Comprehensive \ninstitutional reforms have been implemented, establishing women’s economic \nempowerment as a", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-029", "title": "30", "content": "30\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nAccelerated and Inclusive  Human Capital Activation Employers were also prevented from firing women during pregnancy in 2019, \nand maternity leave was extended from 10 to 12 weeks in 2025. To prevent an \nincrease in the financial burden on employers due to longer maternity leave, \npension reforms also shifted maternity leave financing to social insurance, \nwith men and women all contributing.\nTargeted gender ‑sensitive programs support women’s entry, retention and \ncareer progression in the private sector. Wusool (a you", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-030", "title": "A Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market TransformationAccelerat", "content": "A Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market TransformationAccelerated and Inclusive  Human Capital Activation \n31\nPhoto credit: © AdobeStock / TICAI\nPhoto credit: © AdobeStock / spcXmky", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-031", "title": "32", "content": "32\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nA More Dynamic \nPrivate Sector \nA Shift Away from Public Sector Employment\nAs Saudi Arabia’s labor market evolves, the private sector’s expanding \nrole is creating more ‑inclusive opportunities for Saudi nationals, women, \nyouth, and less‑ educated workers. In 2025, half of employed Saudis worked \nin the private sector, up from 26.5 percent in 2015 (Figure 6). The increase \nin the share of employed women in the private sector is striking. In 2015, 85 \npercent of working women were in the public sector and 15 percent in ", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-032", "title": "33", "content": "33\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nMost individuals moving from inactivity to employment are now entering \nthe private sector, a significant shift for a country where public sector jobs \nonce dominated job creation. Most new entrants aged 21 to 64 have found \njobs in the private sector, in contrast to the period between 2012 and 2015, \nwhen the public sector absorbed most. Between 2022 and 2025, approximately \n9 percent of the population aged 21 to 64 and 16 percent of those aged 21 to \n34 secured employment in the private sector, whereas only 6 percent ", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-033", "title": "34", "content": "34\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nLocalization reforms have been a central pillar for catalyzing initial higher \nparticipation of Saudi nationals in private sector employment. Localization \nis Saudi Arabia’s sector-based strategic approach to nationalization policy, \nseeking to increase employment of Saudi nationals in the private sector by \nsetting sector- and firm size–specific localization requirements, reserving \ncertain jobs, or meeting certain allocation of jobs for Saudis at specific wage \nlevels. These programs span retail, telecommunications, c", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-034", "title": "35", "content": "35\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nAlongside the reforms to the labor market, the COVID ‑19 pandemic may \nhave played a role in accelerating employment of Saudis, especially wom-\nen. The pandemic may have contributed to the employment surge via a labor \ndemand shock,16 as many foreign workers left the country when the pandemic \nbegan. As economic activity resumed a few months later, employers unmet \ndemand for workers, positioned Saudis, particularly women, to step in to \nfill these vacancies. Saudi women primarily entered employment in sectors \nwhere fo", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-035", "title": "36", "content": "36\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nWage expectations in the private sector have also changed. Reservation \nwages for men decreased on average by approximately 12.6 percent, increa -\nsingly aligning with actual wages offered in the private sector. Conversely, \nwomen’s reservation wages rose by 6.9 percent, decreasing the gender gap \nin reservation wages as women began to expect earnings comparable with \nthose of their male counterparts. Women’s real wages increased in 2025 on \naverage by 8 percent, further narrowing the gender wage gap but not elimi-\nnati", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-036", "title": "37", "content": "37\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nBetween 2015 and 2025, Saudi Arabia experienced a notable shift in \nemployment toward micro firms. In 2015, micro firms (1–4 employees) ac-\ncounted for 6 percent of all workers, SMEs (5–99 employees) accounted for 53 \npercent, and large firms (>100 employees) accounted for 40 percent. By 2025, \nthe share of employment in micro firms had risen dramatically (to 26 percent), \nreflecting a significant increase in the importance of very small businesses \nfor employment. Employment in SMEs declined moderately (to 45 percent) ", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-037", "title": "38", "content": "38\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThis change underscores a clear shift in employment toward micro firms. \nThis could be a positive story if greater firm entry rates or increasing entre -\npreneurial activity is driving this increase, because Saudi Arabia has been \ninvesting heavily in entrepreneurship and startup support, but existing im-\nbalances and distortions in the economy could be driving this trend, limiting \nfirm dynamics and growth and in turn leading to a micro firm build-up at \nthe expense of growth (Box 4). What is clear is that there is a n", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-038", "title": "39", "content": "39\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nMicro firms are a vital source of jobs and in-\nclusion. They absorb large numbers of workers \nand can be essential for labor market inclusion \nof vulnerable groups, yet the same structural \nfeatures that make them so prevalent also lim -\nit their productivity, technology adoption, and \njob quality, creating a fundamental trade-off \nbetween broad employment generation and \nlong-run productivity growth.\nMicro firms make up a significant share of \nfirms in Saudi Arabia and are contributing to \nemployment (Figure B4.1). The", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-039", "title": "40", "content": "40\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThe private sector is seeing gains from an influx of skilled Saudi workers \nacross all levels of education, leading to a general upskilling of the private \nsector workforce. In particular, participation of Saudis with tertiary educa -\ntion in the private sector has risen. In 2015, only 19 percent of Saudi workers \nwith tertiary education were employed in the private sector, with 81 percent \nin the public sector, but this has shifted considerably; by 2025, the share of \nSaudi workers with tertiary education in the privat", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-040", "title": "41", "content": "41\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThe qualification mismatch of Saudis in the private sector was lower in \n2025 than in 2015, with more workers having educations that matched \ntheir job requirements. The proportion of Saudis with qualifications appro -\npriate for their occupations increased from 41 to 62 percent for men and 51 \nto 60 percent for women, indicating a decrease in the disconnect between \neducational achievement and labor market needs. The share of underqualified \nSaudis, defined as those who are performing occupations for which they lack \nf", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-041", "title": "42", "content": "42\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nSkills mismatch can lead to a penalty or premium in wages that is not reflec -\ntive of a worker’s productivity. The qualification mismatch penalty for Saudi \nworkers, measured as the earnings loss from being undereducated relative to \njob requirements, is high and remained sticky from 2015 (–15 percent) to 2025 \n(–17 percent). The undereducation penalty is persistently higher for women and \nbarely budged between 2015 (–22 percent) and 2025 (–20 percent) (Figure 15).  \nSkills reforms have greatly enhanced the private sec", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-042", "title": "43", "content": "43\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nA new wave of next -generation skills development reforms can broaden access \nto a comprehensive lifelong learning journey, integrating recognition of prior \nlearning and micro‑credentials to validate skills acquired through formal ed -\nucation and work‑based learning pathways. Expanding firm-based training and \nexpanding high- quality technical, vocational, and engineering training across key \nsectors can help improve job outcomes and more closely align skills development \nwith employers’ needs. To prepare the workforc", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-043", "title": "44", "content": "44\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThis shift is consistent with global trends, particularly with skills‑biased \ntechnological change shifting work patterns. Technological change, glo -\nbalization, and demographic shifts are driving a global transition toward \nhigher-skilled occupations, with manual and routine jobs increasingly at risk \nof automation and displacement.28 The share of employment in routine manual \nand cognitive occupations has declined. Adoption of digital technologies, \nrobotics, and AI has increased global demand for workers with advanc", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-044", "title": "45", "content": "45\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nSaudi Arabia’s rapid growth in ICT aligns with global trends, with ICT \na major source of job creation and economic transformation. Internatio -\nnally, the sector is recognized for its high levels of innovation and research \nand development and leads in business research and development intensity, \nwith industries such as computers, electronics, and optics investing heavily \nin research, reaching up to 33 percent of value added in France, 28 percent \nin Finland, and 24 percent in the United States in 2016.30 The ICT sec", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-045", "title": "46", "content": "46\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nGradual Shift Toward Attracting and Retaining High‑Skilled Foreign Workers\nWith changes in employment patterns, reliance on low-skilled foreign la -\nbor is slowly giving way to higher ‑skilled, better ‑educated workers, in line \nwith Vision 2030’s push for a diversified, knowledge ‑intensive economy. \nThe proportion of foreign working-age individuals with primary education \ndeclined by 15 percentage points, whereas the share with tertiary qualifications \nrose by 6 percentage points (Figure 18). The number of foreign wor", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-046", "title": "47", "content": "47\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nQiwa, Saudi Arabia’s state-of-the-art labor market governance platform, was launched in 2019 \nto digitize employer–employee relationships, enhance private sector efficiency and transparency, \nand deliver reliable labor market data to support growth. It provides services to all labor market \nstakeholders—government entities, workers, sector representatives, firms—across Saudi Arabia \nand provides a range of services. \n         SERVICES FOR EMPLOYEES\n> Resume and profile management. Access to mobile \napp to build a curric", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-047", "title": "48", "content": "48\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nOnce employed in the country, an increasing proportion of foreign workers \nwere receiving opportunities for upskilling in 2025 (Figure 20). The share \nof foreign workers that were receiving training increased by 14 percentage \npoints from 2015 to 2025, which translated into a greater share of employed \nindividuals reporting that their roles were making full use of their capabilities. \nStructured on-the-job training can yield significant benefits, including greater \nproductivity, employee engagement and effort, retention", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-048", "title": "49", "content": "49\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nDynamism in the Private Sector\nLabor market mobility rose from 2015 to 2025. The average number of em -\nployers that an individual has worked for increased from 1.2 to 1.7 for Saudi \nmen, from 1 to 1.5 for women (Figure 21), and from 1.9 to 2.5 for expatriates \n(Figure 22), indicating higher rates of job mobility and turnover. This trend \nsuggests a more fluid labor market where workers are changing jobs more \nfrequently, possibly in response to emerging sectors, evolving skill demands, \nand labor mobility reforms. The ", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
    {"id": "PLATFORM-P179647_b378ec18_7e4-049", "title": "50", "content": "50\nA Decade of Progress: Inside Saudi Arabia’s Labor Market Transformation\nThe reform of job mobility contributed to this dynamism, marking a \npivot in the country’s labor governance, particularly for foreign workers. \nSpearheaded by the Labor Reform Initiative, these reforms dismantled key \nelements of the traditional sponsorship system by allowing foreign workers \nto change employers without current employer consent. The reforms also \nenable workers to issue exit and re-entry visas independently through digital \nplatforms such as Absher, reducing administrative friction and enhancing \ntransp", "source": "platform/P179647-b378ec18-7e40-488a-8327-0cc6f6c5dd18.pdf", "dimension": "platform", "tags": []},
